## Dev Server

**Rule: only stop servers you started.** If a server is already listening on the
port, use it and leave it running: it is probably the user's.

{% for s in my_stacks %}
{% set dev = s | dev_server %}
### {{ s.display_name }}

- Working directory: `{{ s.working_dir }}`
- Start command: {% if dev.command %}`{{ dev.command }}`{% else %}not configured; use the project's dev script (README, package.json, Makefile){% endif %}

- Port: `{{ dev.port or 'see the start command' }}`
{% if dev.health_check %}
- Health check: `GET {{ dev.health_check }}` (if the route does not exist yet, add it as part of the work, or check `/` instead)
{% endif %}

{% if dev.command and dev.port %}
```bash
PORT={{ dev.port }}
mkdir -p .agent-pipeline
if lsof -ti :$PORT -sTCP:LISTEN >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  echo "Port $PORT already in use: using the running server, will NOT stop it"
else
  (cd {{ s.working_dir }} && {{ dev.command }}) > .agent-pipeline/dev-server-{{ s.name }}.log 2>&1 &
  for i in $(seq 1 60); do
    curl -s -o /dev/null http://localhost:$PORT{{ dev.health_check or '/' }} && break
    sleep 1
  done
  # Port was free before, so whatever listens now was started here
  lsof -ti :$PORT -sTCP:LISTEN >> .agent-pipeline/dev-server.pids
fi
```

If the server never answers, read `.agent-pipeline/dev-server-{{ s.name }}.log`.
{% endif %}

{% endfor %}
### Stop what you started

Run this when you finish, whether the verification passed or failed:

```bash
if [ -f .agent-pipeline/dev-server.pids ]; then
  xargs kill < .agent-pipeline/dev-server.pids 2>/dev/null || true
  rm -f .agent-pipeline/dev-server.pids
fi
```

Never kill a process by port alone (`lsof -ti :PORT | xargs kill`): that also
kills servers the user started.

